Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Third Kick Leaves With the Breath
- Story & Context: The opening track establishes the genre’s central rule. Malik programs a repeated three-kick Jersey-club cell, but Elena’s audible inhalation removes the third kick every fourth cycle. At first the missing impact feels accidental. By the eighth repetition, dancers begin anticipating the breath itself as part of the groove.

Iva answers each missing kick with one deadened harp note whose pitch matches the last stable kaval tone before the inhale. Petar remains silent until the sixth breath cycle, then enters with a low baritone-horn fifth during a complete electronic bass dropout. The middle section shortens Elena’s phrases so the missing-third-kick event happens more often, making the rhythm feel increasingly unstable without any tempo change.

The final sixteen bars reverse the logic: the third kick returns, but the first kick disappears. Elena keeps the same melody, proving that the phrase can survive while the club cell changes orientation.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Worn gym floor with three chalk kick marks, the third partially erased by a visible breath cloud motif, kaval crossing the frame, prepared harp string and baritone-horn bell in shallow focus. Sparse spoken fragments limited to “three becomes two,” “breathe it out,” “leave the last impact,” and “start from the middle.”

Track 2: Felt on the String, Heel on the Line
- Story & Context: Iva prepares one harp string with a narrow felt strip, turning a normally resonant pitch into a blunt wooden-papery thud. Malik assigns that note to the place where a snare would usually land, while a live heel drop on the court line marks the end of each eight-bar phrase.

Elena plays short kaval ornaments that begin immediately after the heel impact. Petar answers only every second phrase with one low half-valve smear. Because the harp attack is pitched but extremely dry, the electronic rhythm begins to feel as though it contains a tonal snare.

Halfway through the track, Iva removes the felt. The same harp note rings unexpectedly across several beats and briefly obscures the kick triplets. Rather than correcting it, the band leaves the long resonance untouched for eight bars, then reapplies the felt and restores the dry attack.

The track ends with a heel impact and no programmed snare afterward.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Macro documentary image of a felt strip threaded against one harp string, worn sneaker heel touching a faded blue court line, kaval finger holes and baritone-horn tubing behind. Spoken fragments limited to “felt stays on,” “heel on the line,” “let it ring once,” and “dry it again.”

Track 3: Windows Open Before the Last Reset
- Story & Context: The EP closer begins with every tall gym window shut, producing the driest room sound on the record. Elena plays long low-register kaval tones while Malik uses only isolated two-kick fragments instead of full triplets. Iva leaves the harp almost completely muted, and Petar contributes breath noise without stable pitch.

After thirty-two bars, two windows are opened. Exterior summer sound enters: distant traffic, birds, and faint voices from the courtyard. None of it is sampled or looped. The room simply becomes less controlled. Elena responds by moving into the upper register and increasing overblown harmonics.

At the expected final club reset, Malik cuts the beat entirely. The ensemble continues for sixteen bars with kaval, harp, horn, claps, and heel taps only. When electronics return, they do so with one incomplete two-kick cell and no sub-bass.

The final sound is Elena’s breath after the last note while the open windows remain audible.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Tall school-gym windows swung open to bright summer air, kaval player near the threshold of light, silent drum machine on a wooden bench, prepared harp beside stacked mats, baritone horn held at rest. Spoken fragments limited to “open both windows,” “no reset,” “sixteen on breath,” and “leave the room running.”

